s  and what was going on because she had so  many friends they were dropping like  flies and she we had to figure this one  out and she explained to me what she had  done for nuclear disarmament that as  soon as they had figured out that  strontium-90 got into milk as soon as  they were able to tell mothers that  their children's milk was contaminated  with radiation that was it so she said  well you got to find the milk here and I  said you know Bella it's not that simple  and I had quite a number of interesting  experiences with her including being  with her in Beijing at the International  Women's Conference and pushing her  around in a wheelchair and at the time  George Bush the former president gave us  talk in Japan and made a quip that he  felt sorry for the Chinese having Bella  Abzug run around there of course she was  in a wheelchair so that was kind of an  ironic thing he might not have known  that and she then replied with her own  retort  uh pointing out as she said well he was  speaking to a fertilizer convention  wasn't he uh so she was a she was  definitely a force of nature but in the  end of her life she was really affected  with congestive heart failure and  arthritis and she had breast cancer and  we would have conversations about  whether there was anything after death  and what meanings of what would go on  after we left this earth and I assured  her that I believed in an eternal life  and she said yeah what makes you so sure  that I said you know I can't prove it to  you it's something that I I know I  believe with all my being and then I  said to her you know what makes you so  strong in the face of all this pain why  don't you just go to Florida and play  mahjong if you don't think there's  anything after all this and she said  well I guess I know my mother is still  very proud of me and I said you know I'm  sure we're all going to be connected in  ways through our work and her funeral  was a really a bit of a blast and their  young woman rabbi got up to begin the  funeral and said if Heaven ever were  male dominated she looked upward and  paused for that to sink in we know  things are changing right now and that  brought down the house and then Shirley  MacLaine who is known for her efforts to  communicate across time and space with  those long gone from the earth began her  remarks looking directly at the coffin  and said I of course will speak directly  to Bella and she turned to address the  casket to her left and continued Bella  you always said you were a feminist but  I also know you were basically a You Man  'used McClain stressed the middle  syllable at that moment the microphone  which stood several feet from McClain  and well away from any living soul  fell off its stand the crowd gasped ok  ok Bella I was just kidding McLain  replied Thank You Deborah Davis is a  senior advisor to the World Health  Organization when smoke ran like water  is published by basic books on the web  at basic books calm
 

